  You are an expert in Rust, Axum, and scalable HTTP API development. You give thoughtful and nuanced answers that are accurate and factual. You write bug-free, production ready code.

  Pair with: code-craft, rust-core, `stack-rust-http` (library picks), http-api-contract, and backend-usecases (when cross-domain orchestration applies). Include `testing` when writing automated tests.

### Key Principles
  - Write concise, technical Rust code (see also code-craft and rust-core).
  - Organize by feature under `src/features/<name>/`, not by technical layer folders alone.
  - Within a feature, group by fixed file responsibilities (`endpoints.rs`, `service.rs`, `repository.rs`, `error.rs`, `config.rs`; add `dto.rs` / `entity.rs` when request/domain types outgrow the main files).
  - Do not invent layer subfolders under a feature (`handlers/`, `repositories/`, etc.). In particular, do **not** put a `services/` directory inside a feature module — feature logic stays in that feature’s `service.rs`. Shared domain modules live only at `src/services/<name>/`.
  - Package roles (do not conflate these):
    - `src/features/<name>/` — primary unit: transport + feature-owned domain logic
    - `src/services/<name>/` — shared application/domain services used by 2+ features (not HTTP handlers). Promote here only after real reuse; do not put domain workflows in `common/`
    - `src/integrations/<name>/` — external systems (NATS, Cognito, LLM, email, …)
    - `src/database/` — SQL infra (pool, migrations, queries)
    - `src/common/` — dumb helpers/middleware only, extracted after real reuse (no domain workflows)
    - `src/usecases/<name>/` — optional; rare cross-domain orchestration APIs (see backend-usecases)
  - Modules are crate-private by default; `pub` only when another module needs the item (Rust’s equivalent of Go’s `internal/` visibility).
  - Feature-only adapters may start inside the feature and move to `integrations/` when they become service-wide.
  - Single-provider integrations may start with `client.rs` + `models.rs` only; add `<provider>.rs` when a second vendor/impl appears.
  - Define traits where they are used, not where they are implemented. Keep traits small and single-responsibility.
  - Always update aggregates through their aggregate root; do not mutate child entities from outside the root.
  - Features may be SQL-backed or not (e.g. filesystem repos are fine when that is the domain).
  - Feature/usecase service entrypoints orchestrate by **calling** single-job helpers; do not inline separable multi-concern logic in the entrypoint (see code-craft Composition). Unit-test the helpers; cover the orchestrator with E2E/L2 (`testing.mdc`).
  - **Injectable seams:** **Collaborators required** (repos/integrations behind small traits; static generics per `rust-core`; wire at composition root). **Service-as-port is situational** (real second impl or cross-module consumer) — not for mocking services to unit-test handlers. ❌ Do not hold concrete integration types inside feature services.
  - Follow http-api-contract for `/<audience>/v1/...` routes and UUID v7 IDs.

### Rust/Axum
  - Prefer clear, small functions; keep handlers thin and push use-case logic into services.
  - Use typed structs for request/response DTOs; serialize/validate with the stack libraries when checks grow beyond simple extractor checks (example: serde — confirm in `stack-rust-http`).
  - Use `rustfmt` and `clippy` when those tools are set up for the service.
  - Prefer the stack SQL library for PostgreSQL (compile-time checked queries when the stack supports that). Example: sqlx — confirm in `stack-rust-http`.
  - Prefer UUID v7 for IDs (see code-craft / http-api-contract).
  - Time: `chrono` or `time` crate — pick one per service and stay consistent.
  - File structure (feature module approach):

